  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Kordofan consortium - WASH project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The South Kordofan IDP Protection-Centered Pilot Consortium seeks funds from SHF to provide a coordinated, multi-sectoral response to the IDPs, South Sudanese refugees and host communities in Abu Jebaha, Rashad and Alabasiya of South Kordofan State. Global Aid Hand (consortium lead), Almanar, AORD, ASSIST, CAFOD, CIS, and Lebena will work together to address critical gaps in the three localities. Among the partners, Global AID Hand (GAH) was selected to lead the consortium given its strong presence in South Kordofan and protection focus. These national and international organizations came together multiple times to design this response. Through a multi-sectoral and integrated approach, coordination with relevant humanitarian actors, local communities and local government, and bringing together different parts of the social protection/resilience systems, the consortium believes it will increase impact and enhance return on investment (VFM). 

Global AID Hand will be the lead to implement and mainstream protection. The project will address child protection (such as child labor, child marriage, school drop-out), GBV and general protection needs so as to reduce the impact of conflict and displacement. The project will respond specifically to women, men, girls and boys, the elderly and people with special needs. The response will provide Quick Impact interventions and will establish and reinforce Community Based Protection Committees (CBPCs). The response provide community mobilization awareness raising events including social and awareness modules, networks and forums to support community based child protection, provide emergency child protection lifesaving interventions and emergency kits, support GBV referral mechanisms. All programs will address gender equality and the prevention of protection risks faced by IDPs, with protection at the core of the response to be mainstreamed across different sectors.

AORD and CAFOD will implement WASH. Between the two the three localities will be covered. Improvement in WASH services has a direct correlation to better protection for households. When children are not required to walk long distances to fetch water, GBV is reduced, will have a chance to stay in school and will have time to play. Reduction in water borne diseases impacts the health of children and IDPs in general. The project will scale up water supply and sanitation facilities mainly latrines, showers and hygiene promotion.</narrative>
  </description>
